   "source": [
    "{\n",
    " \"cells\": [\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"55de160b\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# QuickKart Cancellation Risk — Analytics & Feature Engineering\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Big Data Analytics · Spark DataFrame Operations + Feature Engineering (Google Colab)**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## The business problem\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"QuickKart cancels roughly **1 in 12 orders** — but not at random. A courier is already assigned when most of these cancellations happen, which means QuickKart eats three costs every time: the delivery partner's minimum payout for a wasted trip, the refund processing cost, and — the one that doesn't show up on a P&L line — a customer who just had a bad experience and is measurably more likely to churn.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Meera (Ops lead) wants something concrete: a **cancellation risk score at the moment an order is placed**, so the system can trigger a quick confirmation call or a courier double-check *before* dispatch, only for the orders that actually need it — not all of them, which would slow down the 90% that were always going to complete fine.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Nobody can build that model yet. The raw order log is exactly what three years of ad-hoc logging looks like: category names typed four different ways, a chunk of missing delivery times, a handful of orders with an extra zero from a fat-fingered entry, and no single table that connects an order to how experienced the delivery partner was. **Priya and Arjun's job — and yours today — is everything that has to happen before a model is even possible.**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## What we want to achieve\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"By the end of this notebook you will have produced **one clean, model-ready feature table**, where:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- every row is one order, correctly labelled cancelled / not cancelled\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- categorical mess (four spellings of \\\"Dairy\\\") has been resolved to one true value\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- extreme, almost-certainly-erroneous order values no longer distort averages\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- missing delivery times and ratings are handled deliberately, not silently dropped\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- every feature is in a numeric form a model can actually consume (scaled, bucketed, one-hot encoded)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- you can already *see*, from groupBy alone, which factors correlate with cancellation — before any model exists\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## How we want to achieve it\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Two phases, in the order a real analytics team would actually do them:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Phase 1 — Analytics (DataFrame operations).** Look at the data before touching it. Select the columns that matter, filter down to the slices worth inspecting, search for the data-quality mess hiding in plain sight, clean it, then aggregate and group to find the patterns Meera actually cares about — and join in the delivery-partner and region context that isn't in the orders table at all.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Phase 2 — Feature engineering.** Turn what Phase 1 found into something a model can use: cap the outliers Phase 1 exposed, impute the nulls Phase 1 counted, bucket and scale the numeric fields, one-hot encode the categoricals, and assemble everything into a single feature vector per order.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Every step below is annotated with **why it matters for this specific problem**, not just what the syntax does.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"fecbbcae\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## Setup — this notebook is written for Google Colab\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Unlike Databricks, Colab has no Spark pre-installed and no `spark` object waiting for you. The first cell installs PySpark; the second creates the `SparkSession` ourselves — this is the Driver Program from the architecture session, now running inside a Colab VM instead of a managed cluster.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c62df13d\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"!pip install -q pyspark\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f62e8a81\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, Row, functions as F\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from pyspark.ml.feature import Imputer, Bucketizer, StringIndexer, OneHotEncoder, VectorAssembler, StandardScaler\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import random, time\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"spark = (SparkSession.builder\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         .appName(\\\"QuickKart_Cancellation_FeatureEngineering\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         .getOrCreate())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"spark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\\\"ERROR\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"spark\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"e3328f54\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## Generate the raw order log\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This simulates ~150,000 QuickKart orders with the exact kind of mess a real three-year-old operational log has: category names typed inconsistently, ~6% of delivery times never logged, ~11% of ratings skipped by customers, and a small number of order values with an obvious data-entry error (a misplaced decimal / extra zero). The cancellation pattern is deliberately tied to category, time of day, and delivery-partner tenure — the same kind of realistic signal you'd be hunting for in a real log, so the groupBy work below isn't just a syntax drill.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"396419ad\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"random.seed(42)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"N = 150_000\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"cat_variants = {\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Fruits & Veg\\\": [\\\"Fruits & Veg\\\", \\\"fruits & veg\\\", \\\"FRUITS & VEG\\\", \\\"Fruits&Veg\\\", \\\" Fruits & Veg\\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Dairy\\\": [\\\"Dairy\\\", \\\"dairy\\\", \\\"DAIRY\\\", \\\"Dairy \\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Snacks\\\": [\\\"Snacks\\\", \\\"snacks\\\", \\\"SNACKS\\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Beverages\\\": [\\\"Beverages\\\", \\\"beverages\\\", \\\"Beverages \\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Household\\\": [\\\"Household\\\", \\\"household\\\", \\\"HOUSEHOLD\\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Personal Care\\\": [\\\"Personal Care\\\", \\\"personal care\\\", \\\"PERSONAL CARE\\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"clean_categories = list(cat_variants.keys())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"regions = [\\\"North\\\", \\\"South\\\", \\\"East\\\", \\\"West\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"payment_methods = [\\\"UPI\\\", \\\"Card\\\", \\\"COD\\\", \\\"Wallet\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"partner_ids = [f\\\"DP-{1000+i}\\\" for i in range(300)]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"partner_tenure = {pid: random.choice([1,2,3,4,5,6,9,12,18,24,36]) for pid in partner_ids}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"def make_row(i):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    cat_clean = random.choices(clean_categories, weights=[22,18,20,15,15,10])[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    cat_raw = random.choice(cat_variants[cat_clean])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    region = random.choice(regions)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    hour = random.randint(0, 23)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    payment = random.choice(payment_methods)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    partner = random.choice(partner_ids)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    tenure = partner_tenure[partner]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    base_value = max(20.0, random.gauss(420, 260))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if random.random() < 0.004:                      # rare data-entry error\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        base_value = base_value * random.choice([10, 20, 50])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    order_value = round(float(base_value), 2)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if random.random() < 0.06:                        # delivery time not logged\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        delivery_time = None\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    else:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        night_penalty = 12 if hour < 5 or hour >= 23 else 0\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        delivery_time = round(float(max(8.0, random.gauss(32 + night_penalty, 9))), 1)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    rating = None if random.random() < 0.11 else random.choice([3,4,4,5,5,5,2,5,4])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    p = 0.045\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if order_value > 3000: p += 0.35\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if hour < 5 or hour >= 23: p += 0.05\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if tenure < 3: p += 0.06\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if cat_clean == \\\"Household\\\": p += 0.015\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    is_cancelled = 1 if random.random() < min(p, 0.9) else 0\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    return Row(order_id=f\\\"QK-{200000+i}\\\", customer_id=f\\\"CUST-{random.randint(1,40000)}\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               category_raw=cat_raw, region=region, order_hour=hour, payment_method=payment,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               delivery_partner_id=partner, order_value=order_value,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               delivery_time_minutes=delivery_time, partner_rating_given=rating,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               is_cancelled=is_cancelled)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"t0 = time.time()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders = spark.createDataFrame([make_row(i) for i in range(N)])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"{N:,} orders generated in {time.time()-t0:.1f}s\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.printSchema()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.show(5, truncate=False)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"043a8a89\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Part A — Analytics: DataFrame Operations\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*Select columns · filter rows · delete & rename · search operations · aggregate data · groupBy · joins*\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Before anyone engineers a single feature, the data needs to be looked at and cleaned. Every sub-section below maps to one syllabus topic, using this same orders table throughout.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c847c4de\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Search operations — finding the mess before you can fix it\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The first honest step with any real operational log: don't assume the categories are clean. `distinct()` shows you exactly how messy \\\"the same six categories\\\" actually are.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"bcc0b3b6\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"orders.select(\\\"category_raw\\\").distinct().show(30, truncate=False)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"messy = orders.filter(~F.col(\\\"category_raw\\\").isin(\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    [\\\"Fruits & Veg\\\",\\\"Dairy\\\",\\\"Snacks\\\",\\\"Beverages\\\",\\\"Household\\\",\\\"Personal Care\\\"]))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Rows with a non-standard category spelling: {messy.count():,} \\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"      f\\\"({messy.count()/orders.count()*100:.1f}% of all orders)\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"33832ea3\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Delete & rename — standardizing the category column\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A lookup mapping turns every variant into its one true value. This is exactly the kind of cleanup a model can't do for you — if `\\\"dairy\\\"` and `\\\"DAIRY\\\"` are treated as different categories, one-hot encoding later would create *two* separate columns for the same real-world category, quietly splitting your signal in half.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"04ec74e6\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"clean_map = {v: clean for clean, variants in cat_variants.items() for v in variants}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"mapping_expr = F.create_map([F.lit(x) for pair in clean_map.items() for x in pair])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders = orders.withColumn(\\\"category\\\", mapping_expr[F.col(\\\"category_raw\\\")]).drop(\\\"category_raw\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Distinct categories after cleanup:\\\", orders.select(\\\"category\\\").distinct().count())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.select(\\\"order_id\\\", \\\"category\\\").show(5)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"cae4c26b\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Select columns & filter rows\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Pull just what you need for a specific question, and narrow to the slice that matters. Here: which high-value orders actually got cancelled? These are exactly the orders Meera's confirmation-call idea is meant to catch.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"72e3d337\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"orders.select(\\\"order_id\\\", \\\"category\\\", \\\"region\\\", \\\"order_value\\\", \\\"is_cancelled\\\").show(5)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"high_value_cancelled = orders.filter((F.col(\\\"order_value\\\") > 1000) & (F.col(\\\"is_cancelled\\\") == 1))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"High-value cancelled orders: {high_value_cancelled.count():,}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"high_value_cancelled.select(\\\"order_id\\\", \\\"order_value\\\", \\\"category\\\").show(5)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"26885eca\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Rename\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Small, but it matters for anyone downstream reading this table without your context — a column called `order_value` doesn't say what currency; `order_value_inr` does.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"423d2f21\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"orders = orders.withColumnRenamed(\\\"order_value\\\", \\\"order_value_inr\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.select(\\\"order_value_inr\\\").show(3)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"ccba86f0\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Aggregate data & groupBy — where the actual business insight shows up\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This is the point of Phase 1: find out, in plain numbers, what's actually correlated with a cancellation — before building anything that predicts it.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"8b3932ed\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"--- Cancellation rate by category ---\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.groupBy(\\\"category\\\").agg(\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    F.count(\\\"*\\\").alias(\\\"orders\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    F.round(F.avg(\\\"is_cancelled\\\")*100, 2).alias(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \").orderBy(F.desc(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\")).show()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"--- Cancellation rate: night (11PM-5AM) vs day ---\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"orders.withColumn(\\\"is_night\\\", (F.col(\\\"order_hour\\\") < 5) | (F.col(\\\"order_hour\\\") >= 23)) \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"      .groupBy(\\\"is_night\\\") \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"      .agg(F.count(\\\"*\\\").alias(\\\"orders\\\"), F.round(F.avg(\\\"is_cancelled\\\")*100, 2).alias(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\")) \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"      .show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f3f23183\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Joining operations — bringing in context the orders table doesn't have\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The orders table alone can't tell you how *experienced* the delivery partner was, or what logistics zone a region belongs to. Both live in separate small reference tables — exactly how this data would actually be split in a real warehouse. Two left joins bring everything into one enriched table.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c46d20bd\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"tenure_df = spark.createDataFrame([Row(delivery_partner_id=pid, tenure_months=t)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                                     for pid, t in partner_tenure.items()])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"zone_df = spark.createDataFrame([Row(region=r, zone=z) for r, z in\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                                   zip(regions, [\\\"Zone A\\\",\\\"Zone B\\\",\\\"Zone B\\\",\\\"Zone A\\\"])])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"enriched = orders.join(tenure_df, on=\\\"delivery_partner_id\\\", how=\\\"left\\\") \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                  .join(zone_df, on=\\\"region\\\", how=\\\"left\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Row count preserved after both joins:\\\", enriched.count() == orders.count())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"enriched.select(\\\"order_id\\\", \\\"region\\\", \\\"zone\\\", \\\"delivery_partner_id\\\", \\\"tenure_months\\\").show(5)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"--- Cancellation rate: new delivery partners (<3 months) vs experienced ---\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"enriched.withColumn(\\\"new_partner\\\", F.col(\\\"tenure_months\\\") < 3) \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        .groupBy(\\\"new_partner\\\") \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        .agg(F.count(\\\"*\\\").alias(\\\"orders\\\"), F.round(F.avg(\\\"is_cancelled\\\")*100, 2).alias(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\")) \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        .show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"20fa3bec\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### A quick visual, before moving to feature engineering\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Three groupBy results, one chart — this is usually the moment a technical finding turns into something you can put in front of Meera without a single line of PySpark on the slide.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"befd47ea\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"cat_stats = (orders.groupBy(\\\"category\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"             .agg(F.round(F.avg(\\\"is_cancelled\\\")*100, 2).alias(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\"))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"             .orderBy(F.desc(\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\"))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"             .toPandas())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.figure(figsize=(7,4))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.bar(cat_stats[\\\"category\\\"], cat_stats[\\\"cancel_rate_pct\\\"], color=\\\"#E8854A\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.ylabel(\\\"Cancellation rate (%)\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.title(\\\"QuickKart: cancellation rate by category\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.xticks(rotation=30, ha=\\\"right\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"65203782\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Part B — Feature Engineering\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*Outlier handling · null handling · scaling · bucketing · one-hot encoding*\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Phase 1 told you what's true about the data. Phase 2 turns it into a table a model can actually read — nothing below changes what's true, it only changes the *representation*.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"5568933d\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Outlier handling\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Phase 1's groupBy already hinted that a handful of order values are absurdly large — almost certainly data-entry errors (an extra zero), not real ₹50,000 grocery orders. Left alone, these drag averages and, later, a model's learned weights toward a handful of bad rows. The IQR method caps rather than deletes: the order still counts as an order, just not as an extreme one.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"1bf3620a\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"q1, q3 = enriched.approxQuantile(\\\"order_value_inr\\\", [0.25, 0.75], 0.01)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"iqr = q3 - q1\\n\",\n",
    "    \"upper_cap = q3 + 1.5 * iqr\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Q1={q1:.0f}  Q3={q3:.0f}  upper cap={upper_cap:.0f}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"n_outliers = enriched.filter(F.col(\\\"order_value_inr\\\") > upper_cap).count()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Orders above the cap: {n_outliers:,}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = enriched.withColumn(\\\"order_value_capped\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        F.when(F.col(\\\"order_value_inr\\\") > upper_cap, upper_cap).otherwise(F.col(\\\"order_value_inr\\\")))\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"0bb809bb\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Null handling\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Two different kinds of missing, handled two different ways. `delivery_time_minutes` is missing because the order never completed delivery — a real gap, filled with the median so it doesn't distort the average. `partner_rating_given` is missing because a customer simply skipped rating — here, *the fact that it's missing* might itself be a signal worth keeping, so a flag column is added before imputing the value.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"6773f4a7\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"nulls_before = df.select([F.count(F.when(F.col(c).isNull(), c)).alias(c)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                            for c in [\\\"delivery_time_minutes\\\",\\\"partner_rating_given\\\"]])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"nulls_before.show()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = df.withColumn(\\\"rating_missing_flag\\\", F.when(F.col(\\\"partner_rating_given\\\").isNull(), 1).otherwise(0))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"imputer = Imputer(inputCols=[\\\"delivery_time_minutes\\\",\\\"partner_rating_given\\\"],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                   outputCols=[\\\"delivery_time_imputed\\\",\\\"rating_imputed\\\"]).setStrategy(\\\"median\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = imputer.fit(df).transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df.select(\\\"delivery_time_minutes\\\",\\\"delivery_time_imputed\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"          \\\"partner_rating_given\\\",\\\"rating_imputed\\\",\\\"rating_missing_flag\\\").show(5)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"74c5d357\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Bucketing\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`order_value_capped` is continuous, but the business question is really about *tiers* — Meera doesn't think in exact rupees, she thinks \\\"small basket / typical basket / big basket.\\\" `Bucketizer` turns the continuous column into a small number of ordered groups, which is often both more interpretable and more useful to a model than the raw number.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"82238b23\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"splits = [-float(\\\"inf\\\"), 200, 600, 1500, float(\\\"inf\\\")]   # small / typical / large / very large\\n\",\n",
    "    \"bucketizer = Bucketizer(splits=splits, inputCol=\\\"order_value_capped\\\", outputCol=\\\"order_value_tier\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = bucketizer.transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df.groupBy(\\\"order_value_tier\\\").count().orderBy(\\\"order_value_tier\\\").show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"91941985\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### One-hot encoding\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`category`, `region`, and `payment_method` are categorical text — a model needs numbers. `StringIndexer` first turns each category into an integer code, then `OneHotEncoder` turns that single integer into a sparse vector with exactly one \\\"1\\\" — so the model doesn't mistakenly treat `\\\"Snacks\\\"` as numerically greater than `\\\"Dairy\\\"` just because of indexing order.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"fa327a43\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"cat_cols = [\\\"category\\\", \\\"region\\\", \\\"payment_method\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for c in cat_cols:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    df = StringIndexer(inputCol=c, outputCol=c+\\\"_idx\\\", handleInvalid=\\\"keep\\\").fit(df).transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for c in cat_cols:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    df = OneHotEncoder(inputCol=c+\\\"_idx\\\", outputCol=c+\\\"_ohe\\\").fit(df).transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df.select([c+\\\"_ohe\\\" for c in cat_cols]).show(3, truncate=False)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"98d314d0\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Scaling\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`order_value_capped`, `delivery_time_imputed`, and `tenure_months` sit on completely different numeric scales (hundreds of rupees vs. minutes vs. months) — left unscaled, a model would implicitly treat order value as \\\"more important\\\" purely because its numbers are bigger. `StandardScaler` puts every numeric feature on the same footing: mean 0, standard deviation 1.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"d6528b22\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"num_cols = [\\\"order_value_capped\\\", \\\"delivery_time_imputed\\\", \\\"tenure_months\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = VectorAssembler(inputCols=num_cols, outputCol=\\\"numeric_features\\\").transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df = StandardScaler(inputCol=\\\"numeric_features\\\", outputCol=\\\"numeric_scaled\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                     withMean=True, withStd=True).fit(df).transform(df)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df.select(\\\"numeric_features\\\", \\\"numeric_scaled\\\").show(3, truncate=False)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"9baa3460\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Final assembly — the model-ready table\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Every engineered piece — scaled numerics, one-hot encoded categoricals, the value tier, the missing-rating flag — comes together into one `features` vector per order, sitting right next to the label (`is_cancelled`) a future session will train against.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"4e257328\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"final_cols = [\\\"numeric_scaled\\\"] + [c+\\\"_ohe\\\" for c in cat_cols] + [\\\"order_value_tier\\\", \\\"rating_missing_flag\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"feature_table = VectorAssembler(inputCols=final_cols, outputCol=\\\"features\\\") \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                    .transform(df) \\\\\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                    .select(\\\"order_id\\\", \\\"features\\\", \\\"is_cancelled\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"feature_table.show(5, truncate=False)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"\\\\nFinal feature table: {feature_table.count():,} rows, ready to hand to a model.\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"eac09035\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Before / after — what outlier capping actually did to the distribution\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Worth seeing once: the same column, before and after Part B's outlier step, so the effect of a single line of `.withColumn()` is visible rather than theoretical.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"3a6f82ee\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import pandas as pd\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"before = enriched.select(\\\"order_value_inr\\\").toPandas()[\\\"order_value_inr\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"after = df.select(\\\"order_value_capped\\\").toPandas()[\\\"order_value_capped\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11,4), sharey=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].hist(before, bins=60, color=\\\"#E35B5B\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].set_title(\\\"Before capping\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].set_xlabel(\\\"Order value (₹)\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].hist(after, bins=60, color=\\\"#39B87F\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].set_title(\\\"After IQR capping\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].set_xlabel(\\\"Order value (₹)\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"613b3948\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## What we set out to achieve — and where it stands now\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- ✅ Six true categories, not seventeen spellings of six categories\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- ✅ Extreme, likely-erroneous order values capped, not silently distorting every average downstream\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- ✅ Missing delivery times imputed; missing ratings imputed **and** flagged, so \\\"customer skipped rating\\\" isn't thrown away as information\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- ✅ Every numeric feature scaled, every categorical one-hot encoded, order value bucketed into business-meaningful tiers\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- ✅ One `features` vector per order, sitting next to the `is_cancelled` label — the exact shape a classification model expects\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**What we already know without a model at all:** night orders cancel almost twice as often as day orders, new delivery partners cancel roughly twice as often as experienced ones, and Household orders cancel slightly more than any other category. That's real business signal Meera can act on *today* — training the actual risk-scoring model on `feature_table` is a natural next session.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Change-one-thing exercises\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. **Tighten the outlier rule.** Change the IQR multiplier from `1.5` to `3.0`. How many fewer orders get capped? Which is more defensible for this business problem — being aggressive or conservative about what counts as \\\"erroneous\\\"?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. **Re-bucket order value.** Add a fifth tier by splitting the top bucket in two. Does the new top tier still have enough orders in it to be useful, or is it too small to trust?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. **Add a feature.** Engineer one new feature from columns already in `enriched` — e.g. a `weekend_order` flag if you add a day-of-week field, or a `payment_is_cod` flag. Add it to `final_cols` and rebuild `feature_table`.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. **Question the null strategy.** `rating_missing_flag` was kept as a feature on the theory that a skipped rating might itself be predictive. Group by `rating_missing_flag` and check the cancellation rate for each group — was that theory actually right?\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  }\n",
    " ],\n",
